  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Right after Katya storms out of Barinov's office, Elena walks in and recognizes her as the rude motorcyclist from the parking lot, while asking to borrow Viktor's car.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А что эта особа здесь делает?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Работает. Поваром.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Женщина-повар у тебя?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ну, Нагиев навязал, она специалист по молекулярной кухне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "А что ты, собственно, зашла?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Машина нужна на пару часов.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Дашь свою?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ради бога, аккуратнее.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты сейчас за кого переживаешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "За машину или за меня?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Вы мне обе по-разному дорогие.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо, Витя.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А что эта особа здесь делает?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what is this person doing here?' Notice how Russian expresses 'and' and 'does' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Работает. Поваром.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She works here. As a cook.' The meaning hinges on 'works' working together with 'as a cook' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Женщина-повар у тебя?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A female cook in your kitchen?' Elena raises the pitch on 'you' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ну, Нагиев навязал, она специалист по молекулярной кухне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, Nagiyev forced her on me, she is a molecular cuisine specialist.' The '-l' ending on the verb meaning 'forced on' places the action in the masculine past tense.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "А что ты, собственно, зашла?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And why did you actually drop by?' Chef Barinov addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'dropped by'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Машина нужна на пару часов.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I need a car for a couple of hours.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'machine' before landing on 'hours'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Дашь свою?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Will you lend me yours?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'will give' and finishing on 'your'. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ради бога, аккуратнее.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'For heaven's sake, be careful.' Chef Barinov anchors the line on 'for the sake of', while 'more carefully' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты сейчас за кого переживаешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Who are you worrying about right now?' In Russian, the idea of 'you' flows naturally into 'worry' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "За машину или за меня?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'About the car or about me?' Notice how Russian expresses 'for' and 'me' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Вы мне обе по-разному дорогие.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Both of you are dear to me in different ways.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'you' before landing on 'dear'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо, Витя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you, Vitya.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'thank you' and finishing on 'Vitya'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
